nybody can choose  their own price which isn't going to  make me rich  so we're kind of in a bind but it turns  out that there's another one of open  zeppelin's contracts  eip 712 which is a standard for hashing  and signing structured data  and the signing part of that is the  important part  an example use here on the page you can  see that they take some data they run it  through this hash type data v4 function  and then together with the signature of  that same data  they can use another function  ecdsa.recover to recover who signed it  now here's the important part  because now once you recover who signed  it you can make sure that there's  somebody who was allowed to set the  price of the parameters  so basically the parameters have to  match the signature otherwise it's  invalid  so let's see it in action  so i've got this updated version of the  contract at the top i have a bunch of  imports which just give me access to  some of this new functionality  i have this struct here which holds some  info about the nft that's supposed to be  minted so the token id that it'll get  the price that it's being sold for the  url that's about it you can keep going i  guess maybe add an expiration date who  knows  then i've got this lazy mint function  and this has changed a bit  now you've got this parameter for the  nft data signed nft data call data nft  and then the signature  now call data is a keyword that just  mostly tells solidity that this is a  read-only parameter  so most of this code is pretty much  straight lifted from the open zeppelin  docs we hash the structure combine that  with the signature to recover who  originally signed it  and then we checked that whoever signed  it was actually allowed to sign things  so we do this owner call which checks if  the signature on this data matches the  owner of the contract  basically means did we sign off on these  specific parameters  it restricts it so that only us can be  choosing the parameters like price  then the other slight modification is  that in lazy min nft  this is a payable function now so i  could take the money that was sent and  then i can send it to the creator of the  contract which would be me of course  i've gone ahead and written a bunch of  tests for this i basically wrote one for  each check or action that the contract  could potentially take when i run them  they all passed which is kind of what  you expect since i wrote them as i wrote  this  so i checked that the owner can sign  that when a mint occurs the owner gets  paid that the non-owners cannot sign it  or that if you try and change the  parameters after you sign it the whole  thing fails  if we try to shortchange the transaction  for example or buy the same thing twice  you know all the edge cases really  and we can confirm that these tests are  working by going back and let's say i  remove the price check  and then when we run the tests well  there we go we get a failing test  or let's say i removed the signature  validation  we should in theory get several test  failures from that  which thankfully we do the test that  checks if non-owners sign and if you  change the parameters both fail  since the calls now succeed so we're all  set i deploy that using my deploy script  again and it's up and running on polygon  and we can find it using ether scan too  and now you can see here i've mocked up  a quick and dirty page of my own nfts  i've been working on a shader course for  the past few months and these are all  screenshots from projects from within  that course  if you've ever wanted to learn shader  programming from the basics to the  really advanced i recommend it since you  know i made it there's a link in the  description below now we can go ahead  and help ourselves to some nfts  i have really no idea what i'm doing  when it comes to html and css  and i've made about as little effort to  learn as is possible  so this site could look a lot better  but you can click on the nfts here and  it connects to your metamask wallet you  can confirm the transaction  and if we wait a little while well  this takes quite a while actually  sometimes it takes like 15 seconds or  longer  anyway once it's done we get a little  congrats and the nft is marked as owned  i'm just going to go ahead and buy all  of my nfts  then of course if we refresh the page  they all stay owned because i'm querying  the contract now to see which ones are  owned and which ones aren't  so these can never be bought again  and if i go to open c this is totally  there  i could turn around and list it here and  resell it and when you view the nft  look all of our stats and stuff it all  showed up  check it out they've got properties so  i've got astrology sign awesome  personality valuable  we've even got stats i didn't really put  anything inside of stats  but you've also got boosts so whatever i  put in there  description and thumbnail are all  showing up everything looks good  and yeah so that's my little 4a into the  world of nfts  interesting little trip maybe you  learned something too  all the codes up on github so until next  time cheers
 

